.\" Contact fangqq@gmail.com to correct errors or typos.
.TH MCX 1 "October 2026" "v2026 (3.0)" "Monte Carlo eXtreme (MCX) man page"
.SH NAME
mcx \- a GPU-accelerated 3-D photon transport simulator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mcx
\-f input.json [\-option1 value] [\-option2 value] ...
.br
.B mcx
\-Q benchmark [\-option1 value] ...
.br
.B mcx
\-N dbname/benchmark [\-option1 value] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Monte Carlo eXtreme (MCX) is a fast photon transport simulation
software for 3D heterogeneous turbid media. By taking advantage of
the massively parallel threads and extremely low memory latency in a
modern graphics processing unit (GPU), this program is able to perform Monte
Carlo (MC) simulations at a blazing speed, typically hundreds to
a thousand times faster than a fully optimized CPU-based MC
implementation.

MCX is written in C and CUDA and runs on NVIDIA GPUs. The same source code
can also be compiled for AMD GPUs using ROCm/HIP (cmake \-DUSE_HIP=ON).
For other OpenCL-capable processors, including Intel/AMD/Apple GPUs and CPUs,
please use MCX-CL (mcxcl), which accepts the same input files and command line
options.

MCX reads simulation settings from a JSON input file (or a legacy .inp file),
a built-in benchmark (\-Q) or a simulation shared on NeuroJSON.io (\-N). By
default, the volumetric output is saved in the JNIfTI format (.jnii), detected
photon data in a .jdt (JSON/JData) file, and photon trajectories, if requested,
in a _traj.jdt file.

The underlying algorithms of this software can be found in

Qianqian Fang and David Boas, "Monte Carlo Simulation of Photon Migration
in 3D Turbid Media Accelerated by Graphics Processing Units," Opt. Express,
vol. 17, issue 22, pp. 20178-20190 (2009). URL: https://doi.org/10.1364/OE.17.020178

Leiming Yu, Fanny Nina-Paravecino, David Kaeli, Qianqian Fang, "Scalable
and massively parallel Monte Carlo photon transport simulations for
heterogeneous computing platforms," J. Biomed. Opt. 23(1), 010504 (2018).
URL: https://doi.org/10.1117/1.JBO.23.1.010504

Shijie Yan and Qianqian Fang, "Hybrid mesh and voxel based Monte Carlo
algorithm for accurate and efficient photon transport modeling in complex
bio-tissues," Biomed. Opt. Express, 11(11) pp. 6262-6270 (2020).
URL: https://opg.optica.org/boe/abstract.cfm?uri=boe-11-11-6262

Please see https://mcx.space and https://mcx.space/wiki for detailed
documentation and examples. The MCX project is funded by the US
National Institute of Health (NIH)/National Institute of General Medical
Sciences (NIGMS) under grant R01-GM114365 (PI: Qianqian Fang). This software
is written and maintained by Dr. Qianqian Fang (https://fanglab.org).

.SH OPTIONS
Running `mcx` without any parameter prints the help information (same
as \-h/\-\-help). One or multiple options can be used in the command line.
If an option appears multiple times, the last appearance overwrites earlier
settings. The values shown in brackets below are the defaults.

.SS Required options
.TP
\fB\-f/\-\-input\fR \fIfilename|'{...}'|-\fR
Read the simulation settings from an input file. If the file name has a
\&'.json' extension, it is parsed as a JSON input file; a file ending with
\&'.inp' is read in the legacy text format. If the file name contains spaces,
one must quote the full file name (double quotes on Windows).

If this flag is followed by an inline JSON string, such as \-f '{...}', the
parameter is parsed as an "inline JSON input file". If \-f is followed by
nothing or a single '\-', mcx reads the input from stdin via a pipe.

.TP
\fB\-Q/\-\-bench\fR \fI[name]\fR
Run a built-in benchmark. Use \-Q without a name to list all built-in
benchmarks. As of MCX v2026, these include
cube60, cube60b, cube60planar, cubesph60b, onecube, twocube, skinvessel,
sphshells, spherebox and colin27.

The settings of a built-in benchmark can be overwritten by additional
command line flags, such as \-Q cube60 \-n 1e8, which runs 1e8 photons instead
of the default 1e6. Use \-\-bench name \-\-dumpjson to export the benchmark as a
standalone JSON input file.

.TP
\fB\-N/\-\-net\fR \fI[benchmark]\fR
Download and run a simulation shared on NeuroJSON.io; use \-N alone to list
the available simulations. The input can be a dataset REST-API URL starting
with http, or in the format of "dbname/benchmarkname". This function requires
\&'curl', install from https://curl.se/

.SS MC options
.TP
\fB\-n/\-\-photon\fR \fI[0|int]\fR
Total number of photons to be simulated. The number can be an integer, such as
100000, or in an exponential form, such as 1e5. The maximum accepted value is
9.2234e+18 on 64bit systems.

.TP
\fB\-r/\-\-repeat\fR \fI[1|+/-int]\fR
If N is positive, repeat the simulation N times, so that a total of
nphoton*N photons are simulated with nphoton per batch. If N is negative,
the total photons are split into abs(N) batches, each simulating
nphoton/abs(N) photons. This option can be used to keep the run time of each
kernel below the driver watchdog limit on a GPU that also drives a display.

.TP
\fB\-b/\-\-reflect\fR \fI[1|0]\fR
1 to reflect or transmit photons at the boundaries based on Fresnel's
equations when refractive indices mismatch; 0 to let photons exit.

.TP
\fB\-B/\-\-bc\fR \fI['______000000']\fR
Per-face boundary condition (BC), case insensitive. The first 6 letters define
the BC of the bounding box faces at the \-x,\-y,\-z,+x,+y,+z axes and overwrite
\-b if given. Each letter can be one of
.nf
    '_': undefined, fallback to \-b
    'r': like \-b 1, Fresnel reflection BC
    'a': like \-b 0, total absorption BC
    'm': mirror or total reflection BC
    'c': cyclic BC, enter from opposite face
.fi

If the input contains additional 6 letters, the 7th-12th letters can be
.nf
    '0': do not use this face to detect photons, or
    '1': use this face for photon detection (\-d 1)
.fi
the order of the faces for letters 7-12 is the same as the first 6 letters.
For example, \-\-bc ______010 saves photons exiting at the plane y=0.

.TP
\fB\-u/\-\-unitinmm\fR \fI[1.|float]\fR
The edge length, in mm, of a voxel. For example, if the voxels are
0.1x0.1x0.1 mm^3, use '\-u 0.1'. Only isotropic voxels are supported.

.TP
\fB\-U/\-\-normalize\fR \fI[1|0|2]\fR
1 to normalize the output; 0 to save the raw accumulated values. For fluence,
the normalization produces a Green's function, removing the dependency on the
total photon number, voxel size and time-gate width. \-U 2 behaves as 1,
except in the replay mode, where the normalization by the total detected
weight is skipped and only the unit conversion is applied.

.TP
\fB\-E/\-\-seed\fR \fI[1648335518|int|file.jdt|file.mch]\fR
Set the seed of the host random number generator (RNG), which in turn
initializes the seeds of each GPU thread. \-E \-1 seeds the RNG from the
system clock. If followed by a detected photon file saved with \-q 1 (.jdt,
legacy .jdat or .mch), mcx "replays" the detected photons; the replay mode
is used to compute the mua/mus Jacobians (see \-O and \-Y).

A fixed seed reproduces a simulation bit-wise only when the thread and block
sizes are fixed (\-A 0); see \-A.

.TP
\fB\-z/\-\-srcfrom0\fR \fI[0|1]\fR
1: the volume origin is [0 0 0]; 0: the origin is at [1 1 1], i.e. the
lower-bottom corner of the first voxel. All source and detector positions
are referenced from this origin.

.TP
\fB\-k/\-\-voidtime\fR \fI[1|0]\fR
When the source is outside of the domain, 1 starts the time-of-flight
at launch; 0 starts the timer when the photon enters the first non-zero voxel.

.TP
\fB\-Y/\-\-replaydet\fR \fI[0|int]\fR
In the replay mode (\-E), replay only the detected photons from the given
detector (detector IDs start from 1). If 0, all detectors are replayed and
the Jacobians are summed; if \-1, all detectors are replayed and the Jacobians
are saved separately.

.TP
\fB\-V/\-\-specular\fR \fI[0|1]\fR
1 if the source is located in the background (zero voxels), so that the
specular reflection at the entry to the domain is considered; 0 if the source
is inside the medium. The energy lost by specular reflection is excluded from
the total in the absorption fraction reported at the end of a run.

.TP
\fB\-e/\-\-minenergy\fR \fI[0.|float]\fR
The minimum photon packet weight to trigger Russian roulette.

.TP
\fB\-g/\-\-gategroup\fR \fI[1|int]\fR
The maximum number of time gates simulated in a single kernel run. Only use
this option when the GPU memory can not hold the data for all time gates, in
which case mcx runs the simulation multiple times, each recording n gates.
This adds overhead and should be avoided when possible.

.SS GPU options
.TP
\fB\-L/\-\-listgpu\fR
Print the information of all supported GPUs and exit, for example
.nf
    $ mcx \-L
    =============================   GPU Information  ================================
    Device 1 of 1:		NVIDIA TITAN V
    Compute Capability:	7.0
    Global Memory:		12625903616 B
    Constant Memory:	65536 B
    Shared Memory:		49152 B
    Registers:		65536
    Clock Speed:		1.46 GHz
    Number of SMs:		80
    Number of Cores:	5120
    Auto-thread:		163840
    Auto-block:		64
.fi

.TP
\fB\-t/\-\-thread\fR \fI[16384|int]\fR
Total number of GPU threads, used when \-A 0. The number should be a multiple
of 32 (the warp size); a large number (>10000) is needed to fully use a GPU.

.TP
\fB\-T/\-\-blocksize\fR \fI[64|int]\fR
Number of threads per block, used when \-A 0. It should be a multiple of 32.

.TP
\fB\-A/\-\-autopilot\fR \fI[1|int]\fR
1 lets mcx decide the thread and block sizes from the GPU's resources; 0 uses
\-t and \-T. In the autopilot mode, photons are also dispatched to the GPU
threads dynamically, so runs with the same seed agree statistically but not
bit-wise; use \-A 0 to reproduce a run exactly.

.TP
\fB\-G/\-\-gpu\fR \fI[0|int|'1101']\fR
Select the GPU to use, in the order listed by \-L; 0 lets mcx choose. When
followed by a string made of only 0s and 1s, it is a mask of active devices;
for example, 1101 uses the 1st, 2nd and 4th GPUs together.

.TP
\fB\-W/\-\-workload\fR \fI['50,30,20']\fR
Relative workload of each active device in a multi-GPU simulation, normalized
by the sum. For example, \-W 10,20,20 splits 1e6 photons into 2e5, 4e5 and 4e5
photons over 3 GPUs. By default, photons are evenly split.

.TP
\fB\-I/\-\-printgpu\fR
Print the GPU information, as \-L, and then run the simulation.

.TP
\fB\-\-atomic\fR \fI[1|0]\fR
Deprecated and ignored. Atomic accumulation is always used; the former
non-atomic mode raced and lost data on modern GPUs.

.SS Input options
.TP
\fB\-P/\-\-shapes\fR \fI'{...}'\fR
A JSON string defining additional shapes in the grid. Only the root object
named 'Shapes' is parsed and added to the domain defined by \-f or \-\-bench.
For example
.nf
    \-P '{"Shapes":[{"ZLayers":[[1,10,1],[11,30,2],[31,60,3]]}]}'
.fi
defines a 3-layer medium. The supported primitives include Name, Origin, Grid,
Sphere, Box, Subgrid, {XYZ}Layers, {XYZ}Slabs, Cylinder and UpperSpace; most
objects have a 'Tag' field specifying the tissue label.

.TP
\fB\-j/\-\-json\fR \fI'{...}'\fR
A JSON string to modify any setting defined by \-f or \-\-bench, using the same
structure as the JSON input file. For example
.nf
    \-j '{"Optode":{"Source":{"Type":"fourier","Param1":[40,0,0,2]}}}'
.fi
changes the source type and its first parameter while keeping everything else.
When \-f, \-j, \-P and other flags are all given, the priorities are
.nf
    all other command line flags > \-j > \-P > \-f
.fi

.TP
\fB\-K/\-\-mediabyte\fR \fI[1|int|str]\fR
The volume data format, either a number or a string from the list below
.nf
    1 or byte: 0-128 tissue labels
    2 or short: 0-65535 (max to 4000) tissue labels
    4 or integer: integer tissue labels
   96 or asgn_float: mua/mus/g/n 4xfloat format
   97 or svmc: split-voxel MC 8-byte format
   98 or mixlabel: label1+label2+label1_percentage
   99 or labelplus: 32bit composite voxel format
  100 or muamus_float: 2x 32bit floats for mua/mus
  101 or mua_float: 1 float per voxel for mua
  102 or muamus_half: 2x 16bit float for mua/mus
  103 or asgn_byte: 4-byte gray-levels for mua/s/g/n
  104 or muamus_short: 2-short gray-levels for mua/s
.fi

When formats 99 or 102 are used, the mua/mus values in the input volume
must be pre-scaled by the voxel size (unitinmm) if it is not 1. Pre-scaling
is not needed when using these 2 formats in mcxlab/pmcx.

.TP
\fB\-a/\-\-array\fR \fI[0|1]\fR
1 for a C array (row-major); 0 for a MATLAB array (column-major), whose
left-most index is the fastest.

.SS Output options
.TP
\fB\-s/\-\-session\fR \fIsessionid\fR
A string to label all output file names. If not given, the input file name
is used.

.TP
\fB\-O/\-\-outputtype\fR \fI[X|FEJPMRLSTBADUVWQ]\fR
The quantity saved in the volumetric output, case insensitive
.nf
    X - output flux (fluence rate)
    F - fluence, i.e. flux integrated over each time gate
    E - energy deposition
    J - Jacobian (replay mode)
    P - scattering event counts at each voxel (replay mode only)
    M - momentum transfer (replay mode)
    L - total pathlength
    R - RF/FD mua Jacobian (replay, needs \-E), or RF forward complex
        fluence (forward, without \-E, needs omega); output is complex
    S - RF/FD mus Jacobian (replay mode only)
    T - time-of-flight*path
    B - time-of-flight*nscat
    A - adjoint mua Jacobian (needs detector pos. and dir.)
    D - adjoint D-coefficient Jacobian (needs detector pos. and dir.)
    U - adjoint mus Jacobian
    V - adjoint mus' Jacobian
    W - dual adjoint [J_mua,J_D] pair in one session
    Q - dual adjoint [J_mua,J_mus'] pair in one session
.fi

The adjoint outputs (A/D/U/V/W/Q) are computed from forward simulations of
the sources and the detectors; each detector needs a direction
(Optode.Detector.Dir in the JSON input). Both the forward fluence and the
Jacobians are saved.

.TP
\fB\-d/\-\-savedet\fR \fI[1|0-3]\fR
1 to save the data of detected photons; 0 not to save; 2 reserved; 3 to
terminate the simulation when the detected photon buffer is filled. The
detected photons are saved in session_detp.jdt (see FILES).

.TP
\fB\-w/\-\-savedetflag\fR \fI[DP|DSPMXVW]\fR
A case-insensitive string, or a sum of numbers, controlling the detected
photon data fields (the number of columns is shown in parentheses)
.nf
     1 D  output detector ID (1)
     2 S  output partial scat. event counts (#media)
     4 P  output partial path-lengths (#media)
     8 M  output momentum transfer (#media)
    16 X  output exit position (3)
    32 V  output exit direction (3)
    64 W  output initial weight (1)
.fi
By default, only the detector ID and the partial path-lengths are saved.

.TP
\fB\-x/\-\-saveexit\fR \fI[0|1]\fR
1 to save photon exit positions and directions; implies \-d 1. Same as adding
\&'XV' to \-w.

.TP
\fB\-X/\-\-saveref\fR \fI[0|1]\fR
1 to save the diffuse reflectance at the air voxels right outside of the
domain; if non-zero voxels appear at the boundary, pad 0s before using \-X.

.TP
\fB\-m/\-\-momentum\fR \fI[0|1]\fR
1 to save the photon momentum transfer, used in diffuse correlation
spectroscopy (DCS) simulations. Same as adding 'M' to \-w.

.TP
\fB\-q/\-\-saveseed\fR \fI[0|1]\fR
1 to save the RNG seeds of the detected photons for replay (see \-E).

.TP
\fB\-M/\-\-dumpmask\fR \fI[0|1]\fR
1 to dump the detector volume mask for debugging.

.TP
\fB\-H/\-\-maxdetphoton\fR \fI[1000000]\fR
The maximum number of detected photons that can be saved. If more photons are
detected, mcx prints a warning with the number needed.

.TP
\fB\-S/\-\-save2pt\fR \fI[1|0]\fR
1 to save the volumetric output (as specified by \-O); 0 not to save.

.TP
\fB\-F/\-\-outputformat\fR \fI[jnii|...]\fR
The volumetric data output format
.nf
    mc2 - MCX mc2 format (binary 32bit float)
    jnii - JNIfTI format (https://neurojson.org)
    bnii - Binary JNIfTI (https://neurojson.org)
    nii - NIfTI format
    hdr - Analyze 7.5 hdr/img format
    tx3 - GL texture data for rendering (GL_RGBA32F)
.fi

The bnii/jnii formats support compression (\-Z) and generate small files. Load
jnii (JSON) and bnii (BJData) files using the lightweight libraries below
.nf
    MATLAB/Octave: JNIfTI toolbox https://neurojson.org/download/jnifty
    MATLAB/Octave: JSONLab toolbox https://neurojson.org/download/jsonlab
    Python: PyJData https://neurojson.org/download/pyjdata
    JavaScript: JSData https://neurojson.org/download/jsdata
.fi

.TP
\fB\-Z/\-\-zip\fR \fI[zlib|...]\fR
The compression method used when saving data in the JSON/JNIfTI formats
(\-F jnii/bnii or \-\-dumpjson); a number 0-6 or a name, case insensitive
.nf
    0 zlib: zip format (moderate compression,fast)
    1 gzip: gzip format (compatible with *.gz)
    2 base64: base64 encoding with no compression
    3 lzip: lzip format (high compression,very slow)
    4 lzma: lzma format (high compression,very slow)
    5 lz4: LZ4 format (low compression,extrem. fast)
    6 lz4hc: LZ4HC format (moderate compression,fast)
.fi

.TP
\fB\-\-dumpjson\fR \fI[-,0,1,2,'file.json']\fR
Export all settings, including the volume data, in the JSON/JData
(https://neurojson.org) format for easy sharing; the output can be reused
with \-f. If followed by nothing or '\-', mcx prints the JSON to the console;
if followed by a file name, it writes to that file. By default, the settings
are printed after pre-processing; '\-\-dumpjson 2' prints the raw inputs before
pre-processing.

.SS User IO options
.TP
\fB\-h/\-\-help\fR
Print the built-in help of all supported command line flags.

.TP
\fB\-y/\-\-lang\fR \fI[zh_CN,...]\fR
Select the language of the printed messages; followed by nothing to list the
supported languages.

.TP
\fB\-v/\-\-version\fR
Print the MCX revision number and version.

.TP
\fB\-l/\-\-log\fR
Print the messages to a log file, session.log, instead of the console.

.TP
\fB\-i/\-\-interactive\fR
Interactive mode: type in the domain settings in an item-by-item prompt
(legacy .inp format). It can not be used together with \-f.

.SS Debug options
.TP
\fB\-D/\-\-debug\fR \fI[0|int|RMPT]\fR
Print debug information, using an integer or a string combining the
following flags (case insensitive)
.nf
    1 R  debug RNG
    2 M  store photon trajectory info (saved in session_traj.jdt)
    4 P  print progress bar
    8 T  save trajectory data only, disable flux/detp
.fi

.SS Additional options
.TP
\fB\-\-root\fR \fI[''|string]\fR
Full path to the folder storing the input files.

.TP
\fB\-\-gscatter\fR \fI[1e9|int]\fR
After a photon completes the specified number of scattering events, mcx
ignores the anisotropy g and only performs isotropic scattering for speed.

.TP
\fB\-\-srcid\fR \fI[0|-1,0,1,2,..]\fR
\-1 simulates multiple sources separately; 0 simulates all sources together;
a positive integer runs a single source.

.TP
\fB\-\-internalsrc\fR \fI[0|1]\fR
Set to 1 to skip the entry search to speed up the launch when the source is
inside the domain.

.TP
\fB\-\-trajstokes\fR \fI[0|1]\fR
Set to 1 to save the Stokes vector IQUV in the trajectory data (polarized
simulations).

.TP
\fB\-\-maxvoidstep\fR \fI[1000|int]\fR
The maximum distance (in voxel unit) a photon can travel before entering the
domain, if launched outside (i.e. a widefield source).

.TP
\fB\-\-maxjumpdebug\fR \fI[10000000|int]\fR
When trajectories are requested (i.e. \-D M), the maximum number of positions
stored.

.SH FILES
.TP
\fBsession.jnii\fR, \fBsession.bnii\fR, \fBsession.mc2\fR, \fBsession.nii\fR
The volumetric output, depending on \-F.
.TP
\fBsession_detp.jdt\fR
Detected photon data (\-d 1) in JSON/JData; saved as session_detp.jdb (binary
JData) with \-F bnii, or as session.mch with the other formats.
.TP
\fBsession_traj.jdt\fR
Photon trajectory data (\-D M); saved as session_traj.jdb with \-F bnii, or as
session.mct with the other formats.
.TP
\fBsession.log\fR
The log file (\-l).

.SH EXAMPLES
\fBListing supported GPUs\fR
.nf
    mcx \-L
.fi

\fBListing built-in benchmarks\fR
.nf
    mcx \-Q
.fi

\fBRunning a built-in benchmark\fR
.nf
    mcx \-Q cube60
.fi

\fBDump JSON configuration file for the built-in benchmark\fR
.nf
    mcx \-Q cube60planar \-\-dumpjson
.fi

\fBRunning mcx using autopilot mode with a progress bar\fR
.nf
    mcx \-A 1 \-n 1e7 \-\-bench cube60b \-G 1 \-D P
.fi

\fBUsing multiple devices (1st,2nd and 4th GPUs) with equal load\fR
.nf
    mcx \-Q cube60b \-n 1e7 \-G 1101 \-W 10,10,10
.fi

\fBUsing JSON-based inline domain definition\fR
.nf
    mcx \-f input.json \-P '{"Shapes":[{"ZLayers":[[1,10,1],[11,30,2],[31,60,3]]}]}'
.fi

\fBUsing inline JSON setting modifier\fR
.nf
    mcx \-f input.json \-j '{"Optode":{"Source":{"Type":"isotropic"}}}'
.fi

\fBSaving the fluence in the binary JNIfTI format\fR
.nf
    mcx \-Q cube60 \-F bnii
.fi

\fBUse \-N/\-\-net to browse community-contributed mcx simulations at https://neurojson.io\fR
.nf
    mcx \-N
.fi

\fBRun user-shared mcx simulations, see full list at https://neurojson.org/db/mcx\fR
.nf
    mcx \-N aircube60
.fi

\fBPrint in simplified Chinese using \-y/\-\-lang\fR
.nf
    mcx \-y zh_CN \-Q cube60
.fi

\fBUse \-f \- to read piped input file modified by shell text processing utilities\fR
.nf
    mcx \-Q cube60 \-\-dumpjson | sed \-e 's/pencil/cone/g' | mcx \-f \-
.fi

\fBDownload/modify simulations from NeuroJSON.io and run with mcx \-f\fR
.nf
    curl \-s \-X GET https://neurojson.io:7777/mcx/aircube60 | jq '.Forward.Dt = 1e-9' | mcx \-f
.fi

